 ## Overview
 
-The Ray-box intersection method (commonly known as the Slab Test) computes the parametric entry ($t_{near}$) and exit ($t_{far}$) distances where a ray intersects an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) [S2, S3, S11, S52, unofficial]. It solves the problem of determining the valid sampling interval along a ray for volume rendering, enabling early rejection of rays that miss the volume entirely [S1, S12, S31, unofficial]. Unlike [Ray-Box Intersection via OpenGL Geometry Shaders](1768-ray-box-intersection-via-opengl-geometry-shaders.md) which rasterizes the box to generate setup data, or [Ray-OBB Intersection Test](6603-ray-obb-intersection-test.md) which handles rotated boxes via coordinate transformation, this method exploits axis-alignment to perform the test analytically with minimal arithmetic operations [S1, S2, unofficial][S64].
+The Ray-box intersection method (commonly known as the Slab Test) computes the parametric entry ($t_{near}$) and exit ($t_{far}$) distances where a ray intersects an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S11, unofficial][S52, unofficial]. It solves the problem of determining the valid sampling interval along a ray for volume rendering, enabling early rejection of rays that miss the volume entirely [S1, unofficial][S12, unofficial][S31, unofficial]. Unlike [Ray-Box Intersection via OpenGL Geometry Shaders](1768-ray-box-intersection-via-opengl-geometry-shaders.md) which rasterizes the box to generate setup data, or [Ray-OBB Intersection Test](6603-ray-obb-intersection-test.md) which handles rotated boxes via coordinate transformation, this method exploits axis-alignment to perform the test analytically with minimal arithmetic operations [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S64].
 
