 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Parallel Rays**: If a direction component $d_i = 0$, the ray is parallel to the slab planes. The inverse direction becomes infinite. The algorithm must check if the ray origin lies within the slab bounds $[b_{min,i}, b_{max,i}]$ for that axis. If the origin is outside, the ray misses the box [S2, S19, S21, S46, S57, unofficial][S62, unofficial].
-*   **Ray Origin Inside Box**: If $t_{near} < 0$, the ray starts inside the box. The entry distance is effectively 0 (or the negative $t_{near}$ if back-face culling is not desired) [S4, S19, S34, S39, unofficial].
-*   **Precision Issues**: Floating-point errors can cause rays grazing the box boundary to be incorrectly classified as misses. Adding a small epsilon to $t_{near}$ or expanding the box bounds slightly can mitigate this [S4, S25, S31, S57, unofficial].
-*   **NaN Propagation**: If the ray direction inverse is infinite and the origin is exactly at the boundary, operations may produce NaNs. Branchless SIMD implementations handle this by ensuring consistent NaN behavior or clamping inputs [S55, S67, unofficial].
