 ## Background
 
-The slab method for ray-AABB intersection is a classic algorithm in computer graphics, often attributed to Kay and Kajiya (1986) [S5, unofficial]. It is widely used in volume rendering, ray tracing, and spatial partitioning (e.g., octrees, BVHs) to determine the entry and exit points of rays into bounding volumes [S1, S5, S10, unofficial][S53, unofficial]. The method treats the AABB as the intersection of three pairs of parallel planes (slabs) and computes the intersection interval by clipping the ray against each slab [S11, S52, S55, S58, unofficial]. Variations and optimizations, such as branchless SIMD implementations and axis-normalized transformations, have been developed to improve performance on modern hardware [S55, S63, unofficial][S64].
